#include <boost/capy/ex/frame_allocator.hpp>

// Coroutine Frame Allocation System
// ---------------------------------
// This module provides coroutine frame allocation via std::pmr::memory_resource,
// propagating a single allocator throughout the coroutine call hierarchy using TLS.
//
// Allocation Path Overview:
// 1. User initiates execution with run_async(ex) or run_async(ex, alloc)
// 2. A trampoline coroutine is created; its promise constructor:
//    - Default: retrieves recycling_memory_resource* via get_recycling_frame_allocator()
//    - memory_resource*: stores raw pointer directly
//    - Standard Allocator: wraps in frame_memory_resource<Alloc> inside the promise
//    - Installs allocator into TLS via set_current_frame_allocator()
// 3. Trampoline resumes user’s task
// 4. io_awaitable_promise_base (via CRTP) implements operator new/delete that delegate to current TLS allocator
// 5. initial_suspend captures TLS state into alloc_ member and restores it on resume
// 6. transform_awaiter::await_resume re-establishes parent's allocator after child completes
// 7. All nested tasks inherit the same allocator from the trampoline
//
// Core Elements:
// get_current_frame_allocator() / set_current_frame_allocator()
//     Thread-local storage for memory_resource* — initialized by trampoline before any task allocation.
// frame_memory_resource<Alloc>
//     Adapts standard allocators to memory_resource interface (rebinds to std::byte).
//     Stored *by value* in trampoline's promise to ensure lifetime exceeds all allocated frames.
// recycling_memory_resource
//     Default allocator offering thread-local and global pools for reuse of coroutine frames.
//     Accessed through get_recycling_memory_resource(), returning pointer to static instance.
// io_awaitable_promise_base
//     CRTP base class offering:
//       • operator new/delete routing allocations to get_current_frame_allocator()
//       • alloc_ field storing captured allocator pointer
//       • Accessors: set_frame_allocator(), frame_allocator()
// trampoline
//     Entry-point coroutine responsible for:
//       • Being allocated *before* user task (C++17 postfix evaluation order)
//       • Holding allocator (wrapped or raw) in its own promise
//       • Installing TLS in promise constructor prior to user task creation
//       • Destroying user task handle post-execution, then self-terminating
//
// Important Notes & Constraints:
// TLS must be established before first task allocation:
//     The trampoline's promise constructor runs during make_trampoline(), setting TLS
//     *before* the user task is even constructed — critical for correct propagation.
// Trampoline uses global new:
//     Since TLS isn't active yet, the trampoline itself is allocated with ::operator new.
//     This is intentional and safe — only its *payload* (user tasks) use the TLS allocator.
// TLS restoration semantics:
//     When resuming after awaiting a child, the parent's TLS may have been altered.
//     transform_awaiter::await_resume restores the parent’s original allocator from its promise.
// Raw memory_resource* safety:
//     Passing memory_resource* directly implies user responsibility for lifetime management.
//     Matches std::pmr conventions — no ownership transfer occurs.
// Value-type allocators are safe:
//     frame_memory_resource<Alloc> lives in trampoline's promise, destroyed last (after all tasks),
//     guaranteeing it outlives all frames it allocated.
//
// Lifetime Guarantees (Destruction Order):
// 1. Task finishes → final_suspend yields control back to trampoline
// 2. Trampoline executes completion handlers
// 3. Trampoline destroys user task handle (frame deallocated using TLS allocator)
// 4. Trampoline's final_suspend uses suspend_never → triggers self-destruction
// 5. Trampoline's promise (holding frame_memory_resource<Alloc>) destroyed last
//
// Ensures frame_memory_resource<Alloc> remains alive until *all* associated frames are freed.

